1
00:00:09,634 --> 00:00:15,392
This is Rein Room, a browser based platform for teaching reinforcement learning.

2
00:00:15,142 --> 00:00:19,361
The Guide tab introduces the platform and its learning path.

3
00:00:19,111 --> 00:00:23,770
The Games tab lets you browse and load different training environments.

4
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:27,973
The visual guide can also trace motion across the interface.

5
00:00:27,723 --> 00:00:33,012
At the top, you can load a game, pause, and control training speed.

6
00:00:32,762 --> 00:00:37,376
The left panel is the live game environment where the agent is acting.

7
00:00:37,126 --> 00:00:41,670
Here you choose the learning algorithm and the exploration strategy.

8
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:47,878
These sliders control learning rate, discount factor, exploration, optimism, and delay.

9
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:54,526
You can switch between Q Table, D Q N, epsilon greedy, and softmax modes.

10
00:00:54,276 --> 00:00:58,725
The pointer can mark exact clicks when a step needs emphasis.

11
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:02,784
These four charts summarize rewards and steps over time.

12
00:01:02,534 --> 00:01:08,307
The analysis panel reports coverage, action counts, and model sync status.

13
00:01:09,759 --> 00:01:14,513
This bar chart compares action values and action probabilities.

14
00:01:18,494 --> 00:01:22,888
This heatmap shows which action is preferred in each state region.

15
00:01:26,541 --> 00:01:31,375
This view highlights the maximum Q value across the state space.

16
00:01:34,337 --> 00:01:39,111
This view exposes the minimum Q value and low confidence areas.

17
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:47,959
The slice chart lets you inspect value changes along one dimension.

18
00:01:51,478 --> 00:01:55,757
The action wheel links each action to a color coded direction.

19
00:01:55,507 --> 00:01:59,561
The guide can speed up and replay a movement path when needed.

20
00:01:59,311 --> 00:02:04,469
Finally, the overlay can be hidden to return to the normal interface.
